Comments are margin notes on a scene's text: a note attached to a phrase, a sentence,
or a whole paragraph, that stays put while you keep writing.

## Adding a comment

Select the text you want to comment on and press `Ctrl+Alt+M`, or use *Format ▸ Add
comment*. To comment on a whole paragraph without selecting anything, place the caret
in it and press `Ctrl+Alt+Shift+M`, or use *Format ▸ Comment on this paragraph*. Both
rows are enabled only while a scene is focused.

A new comment opens with an empty text box in the margin. It commits as you type,
so there is no save button. If you have not set a name for yourself yet, a toast
points you to *Settings ▸ User* so the comment can be signed.

## Replying and resolving

Anyone can add a reply to a comment thread: click *Reply* under the opening note and
type. Replies are a flat list under the original comment, in the order they were
written; there is no nested threading.

Once the point behind a comment is addressed, mark it *Resolve*. A resolved thread
stays visible (filtered separately, see below), and you can *Reopen* it later if the
issue was not actually settled. *Delete* removes a comment; a reply has its own
*Delete reply* next to it.

## Where comments show up

The same threads appear in three places, all reading from the same data:

- The *editor margin*, beside the scene you are writing, showing only the comments
  anchored to what is on screen.
- The *Comments* dock (leading side of the window), listing every comment in the
  whole project.
- The *This document* dock (trailing side), listing only the comments on the document
  you currently have open.

Both docks filter by All, Open, Resolved, or Orphaned, and sort by document order or
newest first. *Tools ▸ Comments* hides or shows the margin marks and the in-editor
presentation only: it does not touch either dock, which keeps listing every thread
regardless.

## How a comment keeps its place

A comment does not remember a raw position in the text. Instead it remembers the
words it was attached to, plus a little of the surrounding text on each side, and a
hint about roughly where that was. Reopening the project, or editing around a
comment, makes Skribisto search for that quoted text again rather than trust a
number editing would have made meaningless. This is also why a comment survives a
save and reload at all: nothing else about the text (block ids, formatting runs,
entity ids) is guaranteed to come back the same way next time; the prose itself is
the only thing that reliably does.

Occasionally a comment loses its anchor. If the exact words it was attached to are
deleted or rewritten beyond recognition, Skribisto marks it *Lost its text* rather
than silently attaching it to the wrong sentence. A comment that arrived through an
import format with no real text position (a heading, say) shows as *No text
position* instead. Either way, the comment is never deleted: use the *Orphaned*
filter in either dock to find it.

## Comments in DOCX and ODT

Comments travel with your manuscript when you send it out for an outside edit and
bring the file back. An export style can choose to *Include comments*, paired with
*round-trip markers* so a returning file updates this project instead of arriving as
a separate copy. See [the round trip](:help-round-trip) for how sending a scene out
and reimporting it works, including what happens to comments and replies along the way.
